window.__DIYDB__=window.__DIYDB__||{data:{}};window.__DIYDB__.data["wk"]=[{"id":"front-bumper-wk","title":"Front Bumper Options for the WK Grand Cherokee","vehicle":"wk","category":"armor","subcategory":"bumpers","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":350,"high":850},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","sockets","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","bumper R&R"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2005-2010"],"tags":["armor","bumper","front bumper","winch plate","IFS","subframe","unibody"],"parts":[{"name":"Warn WK front bumper with winch plate","vendor":"Warn","price_estimate_usd":720},{"name":"Rough Country WK full-width front bumper","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":380},{"name":"Westin Pro-Traxx WK bumper","vendor":"Westin","price_estimate_usd":420},{"name":"Subframe brace kit (WK front)","vendor":"Custom or local fab","price_estimate_usd":180}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep Grand Cherokee WK repair manual 2005-2010, Group 2 suspension: arms mount to unibody frame rail brackets (archive.org scan)","url":"https://archive.org/details/JeepGrandCherokeeWKRepairManual2005Through2010OCR7201Pages"}],"alternatives":["skid-plates-wk","winch-wk"],"summary":"A steel front bumper on the WK improves protection and provides a winch mount, and on a unibody truck the bumper is only as strong as the structure it bolts to. A bumper with proper subframe gussets and a sub-80 lb weight is the right spec for the WK.","description":"## How the WK Front Bumper Mounts\n\nThe WK is a unibody vehicle with IFS. Its frame rails are part of the body structure \u2014 [the factory manual describes the suspension arms mounting to unibody frame rail brackets](https://archive.org/details/JeepGrandCherokeeWKRepairManual2005Through2010OCR7201Pages) \u2014 and the front bumper mounts at the front of that structure. Mount any aftermarket bumper exactly where and how its maker specifies. This subframe is engineered for the bumper loads (crash management) and tow rating, but it was not necessarily designed for the sustained winch loads that off-road use demands.\n\nWhen a winch pulls on a WK's steel bumper, the load transmits to the subframe mounting tabs. For a straight-back tow with a snatch strap, this is well within the factory engineering. For winching \u2014 sustained tension at 8,000\u201312,000 lbs \u2014 the subframe sees significant stress at the mounting tabs. A subframe brace that gussets the tab-to-frame connection distributes this load across more material and prevents fatigue cracking at the tabs.\n\n## Weight Considerations\n\nThe WK's IFS front end has a different weight sensitivity than a solid-axle setup. Adding a heavy bumper to the WK's nose increases front unsprung mass (for components forward of the strut spring pad) and loads the front subframe mounting points. Keeping the front bumper under 80 lbs prevents meaningful degradation of the IFS handling characteristics.\n\nMost WK-compatible steel bumpers are in the 60\u201395 lb range. The narrower \"stubby\" bumper designs (without full-width wings) tend to be lighter and are a better choice for a WK that still sees highway driving.\n\n## Recommended Bumpers\n\n**Warn WK front bumper with winch plate ($720):** Engineered for the WK's subframe mounting geometry and rated for use with Warn's 10,000\u201312,000 lb winches. At around 75 lbs, it stays within the reasonable weight window. Includes D-ring tab mounts.\n\n**Rough Country WK full-width bumper ($380):** A budget-tier option with a winch plate. Heavier than ideal at around 90+ lbs \u2014 acceptable for trail use, less ideal for a daily-driven WK. The finish quality is lower than Warn.\n\n## Winch Considerations with IFS\n\nA winch mounted on the WK pulls its load through the steel bumper, through the subframe tab connections, and into the unibody front subframe. For occasional recoveries this is fine. For a WK used regularly in serious terrain where the winch sees heavy use, the subframe brace gussets are not optional \u2014 install them. See winch-wk for winch selection guidance."},{"id":"skid-plates-wk","title":"Skid Plates for the IFS WK Grand Cherokee","vehicle":"wk","category":"armor","subcategory":"skid plates","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":200,"high":550},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","sockets","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2005-2010"],"tags":["armor","skid plates","IFS","oil pan","transfer case","fuel tank","underbody protection"],"parts":[{"name":"Rock Hard 4x4 WK skid plate (transfer case)","vendor":"Rock Hard 4x4","price_estimate_usd":175},{"name":"Rough Country WK oil pan skid","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":110},{"name":"Fuel tank skid plate (WK)","vendor":"Skid Row Offroad","price_estimate_usd":195}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep Grand Cherokee WK repair manual 2005-2010, Group 3 front axle C200F removal (skid plate) (archive.org scan)","url":"https://archive.org/details/JeepGrandCherokeeWKRepairManual2005Through2010OCR7201Pages"}],"alternatives":["front-bumper-wk","lift-options-wk"],"summary":"The WK's IFS means there is no solid front axle sitting below the oil pan to take the first hit from rocks. There is a factory skid plate under the front differential \u2014 [the manual's front axle removal starts by removing it](https://archive.org/details/JeepGrandCherokeeWKRepairManual2005Through2010OCR7201Pages) \u2014 but it is not trail armor. The IFS crossmember itself is a major exposure point \u2014 it sits low and spans the full width of the vehicle at a vulnerable height on moderate terrain.","description":"## What Needs Protection\n\nThe WK's underbody priorities are different from a solid-axle Jeep. Without a solid axle sitting below the oil pan, the first component to contact rock on the WK's belly is typically:\n\n**IFS front crossmember:** The structural crossmember that the front control arms mount to. It sits low \u2014 lower than the oil pan in many cases \u2014 and spans the full width of the vehicle. A rock impact here can bend the crossmember and affect alignment or bind the control arm pivot points. This is the highest-priority protection item on the WK.\n\n**Oil pan:** The 3.7L, 4.7L, and 5.7L Hemi all have aluminum or cast iron oil pans exposed below the front subframe. An oil pan puncture is an immediate trip-ending failure.\n\n**Transfer case:** The WK's NV140 or NV245 transfer case (depending on trim) sits under the transmission and is a costly replacement. A transfer case skid is worthwhile on any trail WK.\n\n**Fuel tank:** Located under the rear passenger area. A fuel tank breach is a fire risk. A fuel tank skid is a high-priority item.\n\n## Available Aftermarket\n\nThe WK skid plate aftermarket is thinner than the WJ or solid-axle Jeep market. Rock Hard 4x4 makes individual skid plates for the WK transfer case and oil pan areas. Skid Row Offroad has a fuel tank skid. A true full belly plate \u2014 a single piece spanning from the front crossmember back to the transfer case \u2014 is uncommon for the WK, so most builds use individual targeted plates.\n\nInstall priority order for budget-limited builds:\n1. Transfer case skid (most expensive component, highest trail use exposure)\n2. Fuel tank skid (safety priority)\n3. Oil pan skid (trail use frequency)\n4. IFS crossmember protection \u2014 this is typically a purpose-built crossmember guard, less common but available from some custom fab shops\n\n## Installation Notes\n\nWK skid plates use the factory mounting points where they exist. On a lifted WK, verify plate clearance from the driveshaft \u2014 some skid plates require minor trimming at the driveshaft clearance cutout to prevent contact at full suspension travel. Re-torque all mounting fasteners after the first 200 trail miles."},{"id":"rear-axle-wk","title":"WK Rear Axle Service and Identification","vehicle":"wk","category":"axles","subcategory":"rear axle","type":"reference","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":30,"high":350},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","sockets","drain pan","torque wrench","RTV silicone (Mopar gear sealant or equivalent)"],"skills_required":["basic axle service","differential fluid change"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2005-2010"],"tags":["rear axle","C213R","C213RE","8.25","differential","service","identification","fluid"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar synthetic gear lubricant 75W-140 (rear axle, about 2.2 qt)","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":40},{"name":"Mopar limited slip additive (Vari-Lok axles)","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":10},{"name":"Mopar gear sealant RTV (MS-GF44D or equivalent)","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":12}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep Grand Cherokee WK repair manual 2005-2010, Group 3 rear axle C213R / C213RE and lubricant specifications (archive.org scan)","url":"https://archive.org/details/JeepGrandCherokeeWKRepairManual2005Through2010OCR7201Pages"}],"alternatives":["rear-locker-wk","wk-differential-fluid-wk"],"summary":"[The 2005\u20132010 WK repair manual](https://archive.org/details/JeepGrandCherokeeWKRepairManual2005Through2010OCR7201Pages) lists two rear axles, the C213R and the C213RE, and both use a 209.5 mm (8.25-inch) ring gear. The difference is the differential inside, not the size of the axle. Confirm which you have before ordering gears, a locker or fluid.","description":"## Identification: C213R vs. C213RE\n\nBoth rear axles share the 8.25-inch ring gear, so cover shape and size will not tell them apart the way they would on an older Jeep with a Dana 35 or Dana 44. The manual separates them by what is inside and by ratio:\n\n- **C213R:** axle ratios 3.21, 3.55 or 3.73. Units with a Vari-Lok differential need limited slip additive in the oil.\n- **C213RE:** the electronic limited slip version, with ratios 3.07, 3.55 or 3.73.\n\nThe reliable way to know is the axle tag and the vehicle's build sheet by VIN, which list the axle and ratio. The number of cover bolts is not a way to tell them apart, and this guide does not give a count: count the bolts on your own cover when you pull it.\n\nThe front axle on the WK is a separate independent-suspension unit, the C200F (200 mm ring gear), or the C200FE with electronic limited slip.\n\n## Why It Matters\n\nGear sets, lockers and limited slip parts are specific to the differential and ratio, so a part that fits an open C213R may not fit a C213RE. Because both rears are 8.25-inch axles, plan a big-tire build around that size: lockers and gears made for the 8.25 are the ones to shop, and an electronic limited slip unit needs its wiring and controls left working.\n\n## Service Intervals and Fluids\n\nThe manual specifies Mopar synthetic gear lubricant 75W-140 for both the front (200 mm) and rear (213 mm) axles. Rear capacity is 2.07 L (70 oz) for the C213R and 2.24 L (75.5 oz) for the C213RE, plus or minus 0.3 L. For Vari-Lok axles the manual gives two additive figures: its capacity table says add 0.07 L (2.5 oz) of friction modifier to a Vari-Lok C213R, while its lubricant section says Vari-Lok axles need 118 ml (4 oz) of limited slip additive. Follow the figure on the procedure page for your exact axle, and read the label on the additive bottle.\n\nDrain and fill procedure, per the manual: remove the fill plug, remove the differential cover and drain the fluid (have a wide drain pan ready), clean the cover and housing faces, apply a 1/4-inch (6.35 mm) bead of Mopar gear sealant RTV to the cover, and install it within 3 to 5 minutes or clean it and start the bead again. Tighten the cover bolts in a crisscross pattern to 41 N\u00b7m (30 ft-lb), then fill to the bottom of the fill hole and reinstall the plug. Inspect the fluid for metallic shavings when it drains \u2014 fine metallic residue is normal; large shavings or chunks indicate damaged gears or bearings."},{"id":"rear-locker-wk","title":"Rear Locker Options for the WK Grand Cherokee","vehicle":"wk","category":"axles","subcategory":"lockers","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":8},"cost_usd":{"low":280,"high":700},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","sockets","torque wrench","differential tools"],"skills_required":["differential service","basic axle work"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2005-2010"],"tags":["locker","rear locker","8.25","C213R","IFS","traction"],"parts":[{"name":"Locker listed for the Chrysler 8.25 rear and your spline count (lunch-box or selectable)","vendor":"various","price_estimate_usd":450},{"name":"ARB CKMA12 air compressor","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":320}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep Grand Cherokee WK repair manual 2005-2010, Group 3 rear axle C213R / C213RE (archive.org scan)","url":"https://archive.org/details/JeepGrandCherokeeWKRepairManual2005Through2010OCR7201Pages"}],"alternatives":["rear-axle-wk","lift-options-wk"],"summary":"The WK's solid rear axle CAN be locked with aftermarket hardware. [The factory repair manual lists two rear axles, the C213R and the electronic limited-slip C213RE, both with an 8.25-inch (209.5 mm) ring gear](https://archive.org/details/JeepGrandCherokeeWKRepairManual2005Through2010OCR7201Pages) \u2014 not a Dana 44 \u2014 so buy a locker listed for the Chrysler 8.25 and your shaft spline count. It is the meaningful traction upgrade available to the WK. The front IFS prevents a traditional front locker, but a locked rear axle covers the most common stuck situations on moderate trails.","description":"## Front Locker: Not Available in the Traditional Sense\n\nThe WK's independent front suspension means there is no solid front axle to lock in the traditional manner. Differential lockers work within an axle housing \u2014 on a solid axle, locking the diff keeps both wheels spinning together regardless of traction. On an IFS front end, the differential lives inside the front differential housing (the front axle assembly), and technically a locker can be installed in it.\n\nHowever, locking the IFS front differential creates additional stress on the CV axles and is uncommon in the aftermarket for the WK. The Quadra-Drive II system's front Vari-Lok clutch pack is the factory attempt at front limited-slip, and worn Vari-Lok units are a common high-mileage complaint rather than a platform for upgrades.\n\nThe practical answer: for a WK, focus traction upgrades on the rear.\n\n## Rear Axle: The 8.25 Is the Platform\n\nPer the factory manual, the open or Vari-Lok axle is the C213R (3.21, 3.55 or 3.73 ratios) and the electronic limited-slip axle is the C213RE (3.07, 3.55 or 3.73). Confirm which you have from the axle tag or build sheet before buying parts, and confirm the spline count of your shafts \u2014 locker listings are specific to both.\n\n**Lunch-box lockers:** these replace the spider gears inside the existing open carrier without ring-and-pinion setup. Check that the unit is listed for the Chrysler 8.25 and your spline count, and follow its instructions for the shaft retention clips and torque values.\n\n**Selectable lockers:** air or electric lockers stay open on the road and lock from a cab switch. They replace the carrier, need a gear setup by someone with the tools, and an air locker also needs an onboard compressor and an air line to the axle. Buy the version listed for the Chrysler 8.25.\n\n## Quadra-Drive II Vari-Lok\n\nSome WK Overland and Limited models have Quadra-Drive II with Vari-Lok rear differentials. These are factory active-clutch traction devices \u2014 they transfer torque reactively based on slip. They are not true lockers; when the Vari-Lok clutch packs wear (common at 100,000+ miles), traction on loose terrain becomes noticeably worse. Replacing a worn Vari-Lok rear unit with a locker listed for the 8.25 is the upgrade path for high-mileage WKs with Quadra-Drive II."},{"id":"wk-front-pads-rotors-wk","title":"Front Brake Pad and Rotor Replacement","vehicle":"wk","category":"brakes","subcategory":"service","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1.5,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":110,"high":220},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands (4)","21mm lug socket","C-clamp or piston compressor","13mm socket","18mm socket","torque wrench (75-150 ft-lb)","brake cleaner","small wire brush"],"skills_required":["intermediate wrenching","knows when to stop and ask for help if pedal feels wrong"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2005-2010"],"tags":["brakes","pads","rotors","safety-critical"],"parts":[{"name":"Front brake pads (non-SRT8) \u2014 ceramic","vendor":"Akebono / Centric / Wagner OEX","price_estimate_usd":55},{"name":"Front rotors, pair (non-SRT8) \u2014 Centric 120.67042 / 120.67043","part_number":"120.67042, 120.67043","vendor":"Centric / Bosch / Raybestos","price_estimate_usd":90},{"name":"Caliper slide-pin grease","vendor":"Permatex / Sil-Glyde","price_estimate_usd":6},{"name":"Caliper bolt (if needed)","part_number":"5093355AA","vendor":"Crown Automotive / Mopar","price_estimate_usd":8}],"citations":[{"title":"Quadratec \u2014 Jeep Grand Cherokee WK Front Brake Parts (2005\u20132010)","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/jeep-replacement-parts/grand-cherokee-wk/wk-brakes.php"},{"title":"Crown Automotive 5093355AA Front Disc Brake Caliper Bolt (05\u201310 WK, all except 6.1L SRT8)","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/jeep-replacement-parts/grand-cherokee-wk/wk-brakes.php"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Brakes are safety-critical. If the pedal feels soft after a job, do not drive. Bleed the system or take the truck to a shop. Front pads on a non-SRT8 WK are about 90 minutes of work and $110\u2013220 in parts.","description":"Front brakes on the WK are a standard sliding-caliper, two-piston job \u2014 straightforward if you've done brakes on anything else, but the WK is a heavy truck (4,400\u20134,800 lbs depending on trim), so do not cheap out on pads or rotors. Akebono, Centric, Wagner OEX, and Bosch all make decent OE-replacement pads. Avoid the lifetime-warranty bargain pads \u2014 they fade fast and squeal. The SRT8 uses Brembo 4-pots and a different procedure; this guide is for non-SRT8 WKs only.\n\nYou'll want to do both sides at once even if only one is squealing. Pads wear unevenly side-to-side on a heavy unibody truck, and a new pad on one side and worn on the other will pull the truck during stops.\n\nLoosen the front lug nuts before lifting, then raise the front of the truck on the frame rails and set it on stands rated for the weight. Pull both front wheels. The caliper bolts are 13mm (slide pins) on the back of the caliper. Pop both pins out, lift the caliper off the carrier and hang it from the coil with a bungee or zip tie \u2014 do not let it hang from the brake line. Slide the old pads out of the carrier.\n\nThe carrier itself is held to the knuckle with two larger bolts (typically 18mm or 21mm depending on year). To swap rotors you need to pull the carrier off \u2014 these bolts are torqued tight from the factory and may need a breaker bar. Behind the carrier, the rotor slides off the hub once the small Phillips retainer is out (or, more commonly, it's already rusted off and the rotor is stuck). A few hammer hits at the rotor hat usually frees it.\n\nWire-brush the hub face before installing the new rotor. A rusty mating surface causes lateral runout and steering shimmy under braking. Bolt the carrier back on, slot the new pads into the carrier with the wear-indicator clip on the inboard pad facing inward, compress the caliper piston with a C-clamp (open the bleeder first to push the old fluid out the bleeder, not back into the master), and slide the caliper back over the new pads. Reinstall the 13mm pins with a dab of caliper grease.\n\nPump the pedal until it firms up before you move the truck. Bed in the new pads with 8\u201310 moderate stops from 40 to 10 mph, then let the brakes cool for 10 minutes before driving normally.","steps":["Loosen front lug nuts a half-turn, then jack the front and set on stands.","Pull both front wheels.","Remove the two 13mm caliper slide pins on the back of the caliper.","Lift the caliper off and hang it from the coil \u2014 do not let it hang from the brake line.","Slide the old pads out of the carrier.","Unbolt the carrier from the knuckle (typically 18mm or 21mm) if you are replacing rotors. A breaker bar may be needed.","Pull the old rotor (it may need a few hammer taps at the hat).","Wire-brush the hub face clean. Slide the new rotor on; reinstall the carrier \u2014 torque carrier bolts to factory spec.","Compress the caliper piston with a C-clamp; open the bleeder while compressing so old fluid exits the bleeder, not the master cylinder.","Slot the new pads into the carrier with the wear-indicator clip inboard.","Slide the caliper over the new pads; lube the slide pins; reinstall the 13mm bolts and torque to spec.","Reinstall the wheel, torque lug nuts to 100 ft-lbs (3.7L/4.7L) or 125 ft-lbs (5.7L Hemi, 17\"+ wheels).","Pump the brake pedal until firm BEFORE moving the truck.","Bed in: 8\u201310 moderate stops from 40 to 10 mph, then a 10-minute cool-down."],"pros":["$110\u2013220 for parts, one to three hours of work","Restores stopping distance \u2014 heavy WKs feel it immediately","Catches a sticking caliper or worn slide pins early"],"cons":["Safety-critical \u2014 a soft pedal afterward means stop driving until it's bled","Carrier bolts can be tight from the factory; budget for a breaker bar","SRT8 4-piston Brembos are a different job \u2014 this guide does not cover them"]},{"id":"dual-battery-wk","title":"Dual Battery System for the WK Grand Cherokee Overland Build","vehicle":"wk","category":"electrical","subcategory":"dual battery","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":280,"high":680},"tools_required":["drill","sockets","wire cutters","crimper","multimeter"],"skills_required":["basic electrical","battery installation"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2005-2010"],"tags":["electrical","dual battery","secondary battery","DC-DC charger","Redarc","overland","Hemi"],"parts":[{"name":"Optima Yellow Top D34 (secondary)","vendor":"Optima","price_estimate_usd":215},{"name":"Northstar NSB-AGM31 (secondary)","vendor":"Northstar","price_estimate_usd":195},{"name":"Redarc BCDC1220 DC-DC charger","vendor":"Redarc","price_estimate_usd":285},{"name":"CTEK D250SE DC-DC charger","vendor":"CTEK","price_estimate_usd":195}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep Grand Cherokee WK repair manual 2005-2010, Group 8F generator ratings and battery description (archive.org scan)","url":"https://archive.org/details/JeepGrandCherokeeWKRepairManual2005Through2010OCR7201Pages"}],"alternatives":["winch-wk","recovery-gear-wk"],"summary":"The WK's alternator can comfortably support a dual battery system: [the factory manual lists a 160-amp Denso for the 3.7L and 4.7L and another 160-amp unit for the 5.7L Hemi](https://archive.org/details/JeepGrandCherokeeWKRepairManual2005Through2010OCR7201Pages), feeding a single maintenance-free battery under the hood. A DC-DC charger properly isolates the secondary battery so a dead fridge battery never strands you, and the WK's cargo area is large enough to mount a full-size Group 31 AGM with room to spare.","description":"## Alternator Advantage: The Hemi\n\nThe 5.7L Hemi WK runs a 160-amp alternator \u2014 significantly larger than the 90-amp unit in the ZJ and WJ 4.0L. This extra capacity makes the WK Hemi an excellent overland base: the alternator supplies enough current to run the DC-DC charger at full output, charge the starting battery, and power normal vehicle accessories simultaneously without driving the alternator to its limit.\n\nThe 4.7L V8 WK has a 136-amp alternator \u2014 also adequate for a dual battery system. The 3.7L V6 base has a 120-amp alternator; still workable, though the margin is tighter.\n\n## DC-DC Charger Selection\n\nA DC-DC charger (also called battery-to-battery charger) correctly isolates and charges the secondary battery. The Redarc BCDC1220 ($285) and CTEK D250SE ($195) are the two most-used units in this application. Both apply a proper 3-stage charge profile rather than the dumb bulk charge of a relay-based isolator.\n\nKey feature: both units have a solar input port. Adding a 100W roof panel (or a portable panel for camp use) allows the secondary battery to charge from the sun during extended stops without running the engine.\n\n## Secondary Battery Placement\n\nThe WK's cargo area is large and flat \u2014 a full Group 31 AGM battery (Northstar NSB-AGM31, 100Ah) fits in a bracket mount on the cargo floor without consuming significant usable space. Alternatively, a smaller Group 34 (Optima Yellow Top, about 55Ah) fits in a more compact footprint.\n\nThe battery bracket must be metal and bolted to the cargo floor through the floor structure \u2014 not just bolted to carpet or sound deadening. Use a proper battery hold-down strap rated for automotive use. Fuse the positive cable within 18\" of the secondary battery's positive terminal.\n\n## What Powers Off the Secondary\n\nA typical WK overland loadout on secondary battery: 12V compressor fridge (35\u201345W average draw), LED camp lighting (10\u201320W), phone and device charging (10\u201320W), a CPAP or communication radio if applicable. Total average draw of 60\u201380W from a 100Ah AGM gives approximately 15\u201320 hours of run time on battery alone \u2014 overnight easily, with the DC-DC charger topping it back up during the next day's drive.\n\nThe starting battery is isolated from the secondary circuit. A completely dead secondary battery will not prevent engine starting."},{"id":"winch-wk","title":"Winch Selection for the WK Grand Cherokee","vehicle":"wk","category":"electrical","subcategory":"winch","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":330,"high":900},"tools_required":["sockets","torque wrench","wire cutters","crimper"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","electrical wiring"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2005-2010"],"tags":["winch","recovery","synthetic rope","IFS","subframe","Warn","Superwinch"],"parts":[{"name":"Warn VR12000-S 12,000 lb winch (synthetic)","vendor":"Warn","price_estimate_usd":570},{"name":"Superwinch Talon 12.5i 12,500 lb winch","vendor":"Superwinch","price_estimate_usd":380},{"name":"Smittybilt X2-10000 10,000 lb winch","vendor":"Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":350},{"name":"2/0 AWG welding cable (15 ft)","vendor":"Various electrical supply","price_estimate_usd":45},{"name":"Winch-rated fuse or circuit breaker + holder, sized to the winch maker's spec","vendor":"various","price_estimate_usd":30}],"citations":[{"title":"What winch size do I need \u2014 Quadratec","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/c/reference/what-winch-size-do-i-need"}],"alternatives":["front-bumper-wk","recovery-points-wk"],"summary":"The WK weighs 4,500\u20134,800 lbs loaded. A 10,000 lb winch is the minimum; a 12,000 lb unit gives a useful safety margin. The IFS subframe changes how the winch load transmits into the vehicle \u2014 a proper bumper with subframe gussets is required.","description":"## Capacity for the WK\n\nThe WK is a heavy vehicle \u2014 heavier than a Wrangler, comparable to a half-ton truck. A 10,000 lb winch at maximum mechanical advantage (using a snatch block to double the cable) is a 20,000 lb pull, which handles the WK in virtually all realistic situations. A 12,000 lb winch gives more margin once line stacks up on the drum and is worth the modest additional cost.\n\n[Line pull ratings are for the first layer of line on the drum](https://www.quadratec.com/c/reference/what-winch-size-do-i-need). As layers build up, the effective drum diameter grows and the available pull drops. Running out more cable before attaching to the tree anchor increases the winch's effective pull and should be done when possible.\n\n## Synthetic Rope Is the Right Choice\n\nSteel cable on a winch drum stores significant energy under tension. When steel cable breaks, it releases that energy violently in the direction of the broken end \u2014 a serious injury risk to bystanders. Synthetic rope (Dyneema UHMWPE) drops to the ground when it breaks. It also weighs significantly less and does not develop the wire fragments and kinks that steel cable develops.\n\nKeep synthetic rope shaded when stored \u2014 UV degradation reduces strength over time. Inspect the rope regularly for fraying, cuts, or core damage. Replace at the first signs of significant wear.\n\n## IFS Subframe Note\n\nUnlike a solid-axle Jeep where the winch and bumper are attached to rigid frame rails, the WK's winch mounts through the bumper to the IFS front subframe tabs. For occasional recoveries, this is fine. For regular heavy use (repeated long winch pulls, competition use), install the subframe brace gussets described in front-bumper-wk before relying on the winch under maximum load.\n\n## Recommended Winches\n\n**Warn VR12000-S ($570):** The right balance of quality, capacity, and price for a WK overland or trail build. The VR series has proven reliability and Warn's part/warranty support is the best in the industry.\n\n**Superwinch Talon 12.5i ($380):** A capable mid-tier winch with a 12,500 lb rating. Good value for a WK used for occasional recovery rather than regular heavy pulling.\n\n**Smittybilt X2-10000 ($350):** Adequate for mild trail use with occasional recoveries. Not rated for sustained heavy use."},{"id":"wk-coolant-flush-wk","title":"Coolant Flush \u2014 3.7L, 4.7L, and 5.7L Hemi","vehicle":"wk","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"cooling_system","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":35,"high":75},"tools_required":["drain pan (3+ gal)","garden hose","10mm socket","channel-lock pliers","funnel","shop towels"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2005-2010"],"tags":["coolant","cooling-system","HOAT","maintenance"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar HOAT coolant (MS-9769) \u2014 1 gallon premixed or concentrate","part_number":"MS-9769","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":22},{"name":"Zerex G-05 HOAT coolant (equivalent), 1 gal","vendor":"Valvoline / Zerex","price_estimate_usd":18},{"name":"Distilled water (1 gal \u2014 if mixing concentrate)","vendor":"any grocery store","price_estimate_usd":2}],"citations":[{"title":"Fluids and Capacities \u2014 WK Quick Reference","url":"https://www.jeepgarage.org/threads/fluids-and-capacities-wk-quick-reference.10865/"},{"title":"Coolant Specifications for 2008 WK CRD \u2014 JeepForum thread","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com/threads/coolant-specifications-for-2008-wk-crd.3763553/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"All WK gas engines use HOAT coolant (Mopar MS-9769 or equivalent Zerex G-05). [The 4.7L V8 holds about 14.5 quarts](https://www.jeepgarage.org/threads/fluids-and-capacities-wk-quick-reference.10865/); the 3.7L and 5.7L Hemi are in the same neighborhood. Mix 50/50 with distilled water \u2014 never tap.","description":"The WK uses Hybrid Organic Additive Technology (HOAT) coolant, sold by Chrysler as MS-9769 \u2014 a 5-year, 100,000-mile formula. Zerex G-05 is the most common equivalent. Do not mix HOAT with green IAT or with Dex-Cool. Mixing turns the additives into a brown sludge that clogs heater cores and water pumps. If you pull the coolant and it's not gold or pink, flush twice and pay attention to the heater core afterward.\n\nThe 4.7L V8 holds 14.5 quarts when refilling with the reservoir at MAX. The 3.7L V6 is a touch less; the 5.7L Hemi is similar. Plan for two gallons of premix to do a proper flush \u2014 you will not get every drop of the old fluid out without pulling block plugs, and a single drain-and-fill leaves the heater core full of old fluid.\n\nBest practice: drain the radiator at the lower petcock or hose, run the engine briefly with fresh distilled water in the system to push old fluid out of the heater core, drain again, then refill with the proper 50/50 mix. Burping the system matters \u2014 the WK has a bleeder near the thermostat housing, and you need to run the truck with the heater on full for 10\u201315 minutes after refill, watching the temp gauge, until the upper hose is hot and the fans cycle. Top off the reservoir, then check level after a few cool-warm cycles.\n\nOld coolant is toxic to animals and has a sweet taste that draws dogs and wildlife. Catch every drop and dispose at an auto parts store that accepts used fluid. Never hose it into a driveway.","steps":["Park on level ground, engine cold. Place a drain pan under the radiator.","Open the radiator cap (or pressure-relief cap on the reservoir) to vent the system.","Open the lower petcock or pull the lower radiator hose at the radiator \u2014 let the system drain fully.","Close the petcock or reattach the hose with a fresh clamp.","Refill with distilled water. Run the engine 10 minutes with the heater on full \u2014 flushes the heater core.","Drain again.","Refill with 50/50 HOAT premix (or 50% concentrate + 50% distilled water). Plan for ~14 quarts on the 4.7L V8.","Bleed at the thermostat-housing bleeder while filling.","Run with the cap off (or per the bleed procedure), heater on full, until the upper hose is hot and the fans cycle once.","Top off the reservoir to MAX. Cap and check level after 2\u20133 cool/warm cycles.","Take the old coolant to an auto parts store for disposal."],"pros":["Cheap insurance against water pump and heater core failure","5-year / 100,000-mile interval \u2014 once per ownership for most","Reveals brown or rust-colored coolant that flags a contaminated system"],"cons":["HOAT is non-negotiable \u2014 wrong coolant trashes the cooling system","Coolant is toxic; clean spills immediately, especially around pets","Single drain-and-fill leaves the heater core full \u2014 do the distilled-water flush step"]},{"id":"wk-differential-fluid-wk","title":"Differential Fluid Service \u2014 C200F Front / C213R and C213RE 8.25 Rear","vehicle":"wk","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"drivetrain_fluids","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":45,"high":90},"tools_required":["3/8\" ratchet (square drive for fill plug)","drain pan","fluid pump or squeeze bottle","torque wrench","RTV sealant (for cover-removal axles)","shop towels"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2005-2010"],"tags":["differential","axle","fluid","ELSD","Quadra-Drive-II","maintenance"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar 75W-140 synthetic gear oil (API GL-5) \u2014 2 qt rear","part_number":"MS-9070","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":35},{"name":"Mopar 75W-90 synthetic gear oil (front) \u2014 2 qt","vendor":"Mopar / Valvoline / Royal Purple","price_estimate_usd":30},{"name":"Mopar Limited Slip Additive (4 oz, ELSD axles only)","part_number":"04318060AC","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":12},{"name":"Differential cover gasket or RTV (if pulling cover)","vendor":"Mopar / Permatex Ultra Black","price_estimate_usd":8}],"citations":[{"title":"BlauParts \u2014 Jeep Grand Cherokee Differential Fluid Type Guide","url":"https://www.blauparts.com/blog/what-type-of-differential-fluid-does-my-jeep-grand-cherokee-take.html"},{"title":"Fluids and Capacities \u2014 WK Quick Reference (JeepGarage)","url":"https://www.jeepgarage.org/threads/fluids-and-capacities-wk-quick-reference.10865/"},{"title":"Jeep Grand Cherokee WK repair manual 2005-2010, lubricant specifications and axle capacities (archive.org scan)","url":"https://archive.org/details/JeepGrandCherokeeWKRepairManual2005Through2010OCR7201Pages"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"[Rear axle takes 75W-140 synthetic](https://www.blauparts.com/blog/what-type-of-differential-fluid-does-my-jeep-grand-cherokee-take.html) \u2014 [capacity is about 2.35 quarts (75 oz)](https://www.jeepgarage.org/threads/fluids-and-capacities-wk-quick-reference.10865/). If the truck has Quadra-Drive II with ELSD, add 4 oz of Mopar limited-slip additive to the rear. [The factory repair manual specifies Mopar synthetic 75W-140 for the front (200 mm) axle as well as the rear](https://archive.org/details/JeepGrandCherokeeWKRepairManual2005Through2010OCR7201Pages).","description":"Diff service on the WK is a once-per-50,000-miles job, sooner if the truck does any towing or trail work. The fluid is dirt cheap insurance against ring-and-pinion wear, and the catch \u2014 the part most owners skip \u2014 is that Quadra-Drive II uses Electronic Limited Slip Differentials (ELSD) front and rear, and those axles require 4 ounces of Mopar limited-slip additive in addition to the gear oil. Skip the additive and the clutch packs chatter, the ELSD behaves erratically, and eventually you replace the unit.\n\nThe factory manual lists two WK rear axles, the C213R and the electronic limited-slip C213RE, and both use an 8.25-inch ring gear; there is no Dana 44 rear in it. The factory procedure drains the rear by removing the differential cover. Verify which one you have before buying parts \u2014 pull the fill plug first to confirm.\n\nCapacity: rear is 75\u201376 oz (about 2.35 quarts). The front is the C200F or C200FE independent-suspension housing (1.7 L, 57.5 oz, per the manual). Fluid type is non-negotiable: the manual calls for Mopar synthetic 75W-140 in both axles, and its lubricant section says Vari-Lok axles need 4 oz (118 ml) of limited-slip additive.\n\nPark on level ground \u2014 this matters, because the fill plug is the fluid level indicator. Pull the fill plug FIRST. If you can't get it out, do not crack the drain. (You don't want to be stuck with a drained axle and a stuck fill plug.) The fill plugs are typically a 3/8\" square drive (use a 3/8\" ratchet directly into the plug without a socket) or a hex socket \u2014 check yours before buying tools. Drain plug is the same style. Drain into a pan, reinstall the drain plug, then refill through the fill plug with a pump bottle until fluid runs out the fill hole. Add the ELSD additive last on Quadra-Drive II rear axles.\n\nIf your axle has no drain plug, pull the cover (10 or 12 bolts), drain, scrape the old gasket or RTV clean, lay down a fresh bead of RTV (Permatex Ultra Black), torque the cover bolts to spec in a star pattern, wait the cure time on the tube before refilling.","steps":["Park on level ground. Engine and axle warm but not hot.","Pull the fill plug FIRST. If you can't loosen it, stop \u2014 do not drain.","Place drain pan under the drain plug. Pull the drain plug; let it drain fully.","Inspect the drained fluid: small amounts of glitter are normal; large metal chunks are not.","Reinstall the drain plug \u2014 torque to factory spec (25\u201330 ft-lbs typical).","Pump fresh fluid through the fill plug until it runs back out \u2014 that's the level mark.","On Quadra-Drive II rear axles, add 4 oz of Mopar limited-slip additive last.","Reinstall the fill plug. Wipe the housing clean.","Repeat for the front axle with 75W-90.","Drive 20 miles, then check for weeping at the plugs and the cover."],"pros":["$45\u201390 in fluid for both axles","Catches metal in the fluid early \u2014 a warning before a ring-gear failure","Restores ELSD behavior if the additive was missed at a prior service"],"cons":["Verify your rear axle (C213R vs. C213RE) before ordering \u2014 the wrong limited-slip part or shim kit is a wasted trip","Quadra-Drive II requires the friction modifier \u2014 skipping it kills the clutch packs","Fill plugs strip easily on rusty trucks \u2014 go gentle and use the right drive style"]},{"id":"wk-oil-change-wk","title":"Oil Change \u2014 3.7L V6, 4.7L V8, and 5.7L Hemi","vehicle":"wk","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"engine_fluids","type":"task","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":35,"high":75},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","13mm socket","oil filter wrench","drain pan","funnel","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2005-2010"],"tags":["oil","maintenance","3.7","4.7","5.7","hemi"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar oil filter (3.7L / 4.7L 2005-2007)","part_number":"5281090AB","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":10},{"name":"Mopar oil filter (4.7L / 5.7L / 6.1L 2008+, 22mm thread)","part_number":"4884899AC","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":12},{"name":"5W-20 conventional motor oil (6 qt \u2014 3.7L / 4.7L)","vendor":"Valvoline / Mobil / Pennzoil","price_estimate_usd":28},{"name":"5W-20 conventional motor oil (7 qt \u2014 5.7L Hemi)","vendor":"Valvoline / Mobil / Pennzoil","price_estimate_usd":32}],"citations":[{"title":"Mopar 4884899AC Oil Filter \u2014 4.7L / 5.7L / 6.1L (2008+, 22mm)","url":"https://www.jeepworld.com/products/4-7l-5-7l-and-6-1l-oil-filter-4884899ac"},{"title":"Fluids and Capacities \u2014 WK Quick Reference","url":"https://www.jeepgarage.org/threads/fluids-and-capacities-wk-quick-reference.10865/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The 3.7L and 4.7L take 6 quarts of 5W-20; [the 5.7L Hemi takes 7 quarts](https://www.jeepgarage.org/threads/fluids-and-capacities-wk-quick-reference.10865/). All three are factory-spec on 5W-20 conventional \u2014 but if you live somewhere hot or the engine has miles on it, 5W-30 is fine.","description":"The WK's three gas engines share an oil weight (5W-20 from the factory) but split capacities and \u2014 critically \u2014 filters. Pre-2008 trucks use the 5281090 filter; 2008-and-later 4.7L, 5.7L, and 6.1L engines moved to a 22mm thread and [use the 4884899 filter](https://www.jeepworld.com/products/4-7l-5-7l-and-6-1l-oil-filter-4884899ac). They are not interchangeable. Cross-reference your year before you buy.\n\nThe 5.7L Hemi has a known reputation for soft cam lobes and lifter failure (the \"Hemi tick\"), and oil quality matters more on that engine than on the 3.7L or 4.7L. Stick to a real filter \u2014 Mopar OE, WIX, Fram Ultra, or Royal Purple \u2014 and consider a synthetic blend or full synthetic at 5,000-mile intervals if the truck does any towing or sees Phoenix summers. The 4.7L PowerTech inherits the WJ's sludge tendency; do not skip changes past 5,000 miles on that engine.\n\nWarm the engine for five minutes so the oil flows. Lift the front with a floor jack at the crossmember and set it on stands rated for the truck's weight (a Hemi WK is around 4,750 lbs). Drain plug is 13mm on all three engines; back it out by hand the last few turns so it doesn't drop into the pan. Filter is on the driver-side of the block \u2014 accessible from below, tight on the 4.7L, cleaner on the 3.7L and 5.7L. Oil the new filter's gasket, hand-thread it on, snug a quarter-turn past contact. Don't gorilla it.\n\nRefill from the top (6 quarts on 3.7/4.7, 7 quarts on 5.7), run for 30 seconds, check the dipstick, top off.","steps":["Warm the engine for 5 minutes, then shut it down.","Lift the front on the crossmember, set jack stands rated for at least 5,000 lbs.","Position the drain pan, remove the 13mm drain plug \u2014 back the last few turns out by hand.","Let the oil drain fully (5\u201310 minutes).","Remove the oil filter \u2014 driver side of the engine, accessible from below.","Oil the new filter's gasket, hand-thread it on, snug a quarter-turn past contact.","Reinstall drain plug \u2014 torque to 25 ft-lbs.","Lower the truck. Refill: 6 qt for 3.7L or 4.7L, 7 qt for 5.7L Hemi.","Run for 30 seconds, check for leaks at the filter and plug, check dipstick.","Top off as needed. Note odometer for next interval."],"pros":["$35\u201345 in parts on the 3.7L or 4.7L; $50\u201375 on the 5.7L Hemi","Under an hour with stands and a drain pan","Catches sludge or metal flakes early \u2014 cut the filter open on every other change"],"cons":["Two different filters across the WK production run (pre-2008 vs. 2008+)","4.7L filter location is cramped","5.7L Hemi takes the most oil and the most-expensive filter"]},{"id":"wk-spark-plugs-hemi-wk","title":"Spark Plug Replacement \u2014 5.7L Hemi (16 Plugs)","vehicle":"wk","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"ignition","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1.5,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":90,"high":160},"tools_required":["5/8\" spark plug socket","3/8\" ratchet","torque wrench (10-25 ft-lb range)","swivel/wobble extension","dielectric grease","anti-seize (optional)","compressed air"],"skills_required":["intermediate wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2005-2010"],"tags":["spark-plugs","hemi","5.7","ignition","coil-on-plug","maintenance"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar / Champion spark plug (5.7L Hemi, qty 16)","part_number":"SPRE14MCC4 / Champion RE14MCC4","vendor":"Mopar / Champion","price_estimate_usd":90},{"name":"NGK V-Power spark plug TR55 (alternate, qty 16)","part_number":"NGK 92174 / TR55","vendor":"NGK","price_estimate_usd":80},{"name":"Dielectric grease (small tube)","vendor":"Permatex","price_estimate_usd":5}],"citations":[{"title":"JeepSpecs \u2014 WK Grand Cherokee Spark Plug Spec","url":"https://jeepspecs.com/models/jeep-grand-cherokee/jeep-grand-cherokee-wk/spark-plug/"},{"title":"How To: Change the Spark Plugs on an 07 WK 5.7L \u2014 JeepGarage","url":"https://www.jeepgarage.org/threads/how-to-change-the-spark-plugs-on-an-07-wk-5-7l.16730/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"[The 5.7L Hemi has 16 spark plugs](https://www.jeepgarage.org/threads/how-to-change-the-spark-plugs-on-an-07-wk-5-7l.16730/) \u2014 two per cylinder. [Gap them to 0.045\", torque to 13 ft-lbs](https://jeepspecs.com/models/jeep-grand-cherokee/jeep-grand-cherokee-wk/spark-plug/), and replace all 16 even if only one is fouled.","description":"Yes, sixteen. The Hemi's dual-plug design fires two plugs per combustion event for better burn and lower emissions. You don't get to skip half of them when one starts misfiring. There are eight coils \u2014 one per cylinder \u2014 and each coil fires both plugs on its cylinder through one boot direct to one plug and a small lead to the second. The driver-side plugs are manageable. The passenger-side plugs sit forward of the exhaust manifold heat shields and can fight you.\n\nPlug spec: Champion RE14MCC4 (Mopar p/n SPRE14MCC4) is the OE plug. NGK TR55 (part 92174) is a common drop-in alternate that forum users have run without issue. Gap is 0.045\". Torque is 13 ft-lbs \u2014 these are tapered-seat plugs, not crush-washer, and they will strip a head if you crank them down on feel. Use a torque wrench.\n\nPull the engine cover. Each coil is held by one 8mm bolt and one connector with a red lock tab. Lift the coil straight up; if it fights, twist it gently \u2014 there's a rubber boot grabbing the plug. The second plug on each cylinder sits below a short wire lead with its own boot. Pull that boot too. Blow compressed air around each plug well before removal so dirt doesn't fall into the cylinder.\n\nPlugs come out with a 5/8\" plug socket on a 3-inch extension and a swivel \u2014 the passenger-side rears need the swivel to clear the manifold. Inspect the old plugs (a quick read on running condition) and install the new ones with a small dab of anti-seize on the threads if you live in a salt state. Gap is set from the factory but verify with a feeler gauge. Torque to 13 ft-lbs. Dielectric grease on the boot interiors helps the next removal in 100K miles.","steps":["Disconnect the battery negative terminal.","Pull the engine cover.","Blow compressed air around each plug well to clear dirt.","Unbolt the first coil (8mm), unplug it, and lift it straight up.","Disconnect the second plug's small boot and lead on that cylinder.","Remove both plugs with a 5/8\" plug socket \u2014 verify gap at 0.045\" on the replacements.","Apply anti-seize sparingly to the threads (optional, but smart in salt climates).","Hand-thread the new plug to seat it, then torque to 13 ft-lbs.","Dielectric grease the inside of each boot, reseat the coil and the second-plug lead.","Reconnect the coil connector and the 8mm bolt \u2014 snug, don't crank.","Repeat for the remaining seven cylinders. Reattach battery.","Start the truck and confirm a clean idle."],"pros":["$90\u2013160 in parts for all 16 plugs","Cures rough idle, light misfire codes, and reduced fuel economy","Catches a fouled cylinder before it puts a misfire code on the dash"],"cons":["16 plugs is twice the work of a typical V8 plug change","Passenger-side rear plugs are tight against the manifold","Tapered seat means overtightening will strip aluminum head threads \u2014 torque is not optional"]},{"id":"recovery-gear-wk","title":"Recovery Gear Kit for the WK Grand Cherokee","vehicle":"wk","category":"recovery","subcategory":"recovery kit","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":280,"high":700},"tools_required":[],"skills_required":["recovery rigging basics"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2005-2010"],"tags":["recovery","recovery kit","snatch strap","traction boards","Hi-Lift","shackles","MaxTrax"],"parts":[{"name":"ARB Snatch Strap 30,000 lb (9m)","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":75},{"name":"Warn 30,000 lb snatch strap","vendor":"Warn","price_estimate_usd":65},{"name":"MaxTrax MKII recovery boards (pair)","vendor":"MaxTrax","price_estimate_usd":285},{"name":"ARB Recovery Boards (pair)","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":195},{"name":"Warn 3/4\" D-ring shackle (pair)","vendor":"Warn","price_estimate_usd":28},{"name":"Hi-Lift HL-484 48\" jack","vendor":"Hi-Lift","price_estimate_usd":95},{"name":"Hi-Lift Jack-Mate bumper adapter","vendor":"Hi-Lift","price_estimate_usd":38}],"citations":[{"title":"Hi-Lift Cast/Steel Jack specifications (rated load 4,660 lb) \u2014 Hi-Lift Jack Co.","url":"https://hi-lift.com/hi-lift-jacks/cast-steel/"}],"alternatives":["recovery-points-wk","winch-wk"],"summary":"The WK at 4,500\u20134,800 lbs loaded is a heavy SUV \u2014 recovery gear must be rated for that weight. A snatch strap, traction boards, shackles, and a Hi-Lift jack cover the majority of situations a trail WK will face \u2014 [a Hi-Lift Cast/Steel jack carries a 4,660 lb rated load](https://hi-lift.com/hi-lift-jacks/cast-steel/), and it lifts a corner, not the whole truck.","description":"## Why Recovery Gear Matters for the WK\n\nThe WK is frequently marketed as an \"overlanding\" or \"light trail\" vehicle \u2014 capable on moderate terrain but not a dedicated rock crawler. This means it often gets taken into terrain where it can get stuck (soft sand, mud, deep snow) without the same level of winch and armor preparation as a purpose-built trail rig. A basic recovery kit is insurance for exactly those situations.\n\nThe WK's large cargo area makes carrying a full recovery kit straightforward. There is no reason to leave recovery gear at home when there is room for it.\n\n## The Essential Kit\n\n**Snatch strap:** A 30,000 lb rated kinetic recovery rope or snatch strap. Sized at approximately 6x the vehicle weight \u2014 4,800 lbs x 6 = 28,800 lbs minimum, making 30,000 lb the correct class. ARB and Warn both offer quality straps in this range. A kinetic rope (with stretch designed in) is preferable to a tow strap for snatch recoveries \u2014 the energy stored in the stretch softens the shock to both vehicles.\n\n**Traction boards (pair):** MaxTrax MKII ($285 per pair) or ARB Recovery Boards ($195 per pair). For a 4,800 lb WK, only full-size boards designed for the vehicle weight class provide adequate support. Traction boards are used for spinning tires (sand, mud, snow) where the winch is overkill \u2014 they allow a solo recovery without another vehicle.\n\n**Shackles:** Two 3/4\" rated D-ring shackles (4.75 tons WLL minimum). Screwed-pin shackles, moused with wire or a safety clip to prevent unscrewing under dynamic load. These attach the strap to recovery points.\n\n**Hi-Lift jack:** For changing tires in terrain where a floor jack won't work, or for lifting the vehicle to place traction boards under a buried wheel. The Hi-Lift HL-484 (48\" model) is the standard choice. The WK's IFS front bumper area does not always have a clean Hi-Lift mounting point \u2014 a dedicated Hi-Lift bumper adapter or a frame board (a flat steel plate) used as a base distributes the jack load. On a WK with an aftermarket steel bumper, the bumper often has a Hi-Lift slot built in.\n\n**Recovery strap storage:** Keep straps clean and dry. A coiled strap in a wet storage bag develops mold and can lose strength over time. Store in a breathable bag or dry box.\n\n## What a Winch Adds\n\nThe items above handle situations where another vehicle is available for a snatch, or where traction boards get you out. A winch handles the solo stuck \u2014 no second vehicle, stuck on a sidehill, winch to a tree. The WK is increasingly used by solo overlanders where a winch is the difference between self-rescue and waiting for help. See winch-wk for selection guidance."},{"id":"recovery-points-wk","title":"Recovery Points for the WK Grand Cherokee","vehicle":"wk","category":"recovery","subcategory":"recovery points","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":30,"high":280},"tools_required":["torque wrench","sockets","wire brush"],"skills_required":["recovery rigging basics"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2005-2010"],"tags":["recovery","recovery points","tow hooks","IFS","D-rings","receiver hitch","unibody"],"parts":[{"name":"3/4\" rated D-ring shackle (pair)","vendor":"Warn or ARB","price_estimate_usd":28},{"name":"Receiver-mount D-ring adapter (2\")","vendor":"Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":35},{"name":"Front bumper D-ring tabs (WK specific)","vendor":"Warn WK bumper","price_estimate_usd":720}],"citations":[{"title":"Factor 55 HitchLink 2.0 receiver shackle mount (rating and use limits) \u2014 Quadratec","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/products/92135_905X_PG.htm"}],"alternatives":["winch-wk","recovery-gear-wk","front-bumper-wk"],"summary":"The WK's unibody front subframe carries the bumper and recovery hardware \u2014 not solid frame rails as on a truck. The factory front tow hooks are valid for straight pulls. Rear Class III receiver with a D-ring adapter is the rear attachment. Nothing else is a recovery point.","description":"## Front Recovery on the WK\n\nThe WK factory front tow hooks attach to the front subframe \u2014 the forward section of the unibody that also carries the IFS crossmember and bumper. These hooks are rated for the towing loads specified in the owner's manual and are valid for straight-back recovery pulls.\n\nInspect the tow hooks and their mounting hardware before relying on them. Like all WK unibody components, they can rust on high-mileage northern vehicles. Probe the mounting tab area with a screwdriver \u2014 solid metal is required.\n\nFor a more robust front recovery setup, an aftermarket steel bumper with integrated D-ring tabs (see front-bumper-wk) distributes the recovery load across more of the front subframe structure and provides a cleaner, rated attachment point. The D-ring tabs on a Warn or similar bumper are significantly easier to attach a shackle to quickly on the trail than the factory hook.\n\n**Never attach to the IFS control arms, struts, or sway bar end links** \u2014 these are not recovery attachment points and can fail catastrophically under recovery loads.\n\n## Rear Recovery on the WK\n\nThe Class III 2\" receiver hitch receiver is the designed rear recovery attachment. Use a receiver-mount D-ring adapter retained with a hitch pin. Do not use the ball mount for recovery \u2014 the ball shears off under lateral recovery loads.\n\nThe receiver's tongue-weight figure is a static towing number, not a recovery rating. Use a rated receiver shackle mount and follow its limits \u2014 [Factor 55's HitchLink, for example, is rated at 9,500 lbs with the instructions never to exceed the factory receiver's pull rating, to use a quality steel 5/8-inch hitch pin, and to pull in line with the hitch without side-loading](https://www.quadratec.com/products/92135_905X_PG.htm). Keep snatch-strap recoveries off the receiver to straight or near-straight pulls. For steep, angled, or stuck-deep situations, the front winch is the preferred tool.\n\n## What Is Not a Recovery Point\n\n- Rear tailgate or liftgate hinges\n- Spare tire carrier or spare tire\n- Plastic bumper cover or valance\n- Suspension components (control arms, sway bar, struts)\n- Body panels or floor pan\n\nUse rated hardware only. 3/4\" shackles rated at 4.75 tons WLL minimum for a 4,800 lb loaded WK. Inspect straps and shackles before each trail day for cuts, corrosion, or deformation."},{"id":"alignment-wk","title":"Alignment After Lift on the IFS WK Grand Cherokee","vehicle":"wk","category":"steering","subcategory":"alignment","type":"task","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":80,"high":150},"tools_required":["alignment shop equipment (not DIY)"],"skills_required":["alignment shop visit","basic suspension knowledge"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2005-2010"],"tags":["alignment","IFS","caster","camber","toe","lift","tire wear","handling"],"parts":[{"name":"Alignment (4-wheel, Hunter rack)","vendor":"Local alignment shop","price_estimate_usd":100}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep Grand Cherokee WK repair manual 2005-2010, Group 2 front alignment specifications (archive.org scan)","url":"https://archive.org/details/JeepGrandCherokeeWKRepairManual2005Through2010OCR7201Pages"}],"alternatives":["lift-options-wk","uca-wk","death-wobble-wk"],"summary":"After any suspension modification on the WK \u2014 lift, control arm swap, strut replacement, even just replacing a strut on one side \u2014 a 4-wheel alignment is required. This is not optional maintenance; misalignment on an IFS vehicle causes tire wear that is expensive and handling problems that are dangerous.","description":"## Why IFS Alignment Is Critical\n\nOn a solid-axle Jeep, a basic lift kit typically affects caster angle and track bar geometry. The adjustments are important but the range of misalignment from a simple lift is generally moderate. The IFS WK is different: the front geometry includes caster, camber, and toe angles that are all affected when the strut height changes or the control arm position changes. Even a 1.5\" spacer lift will shift these angles measurably from their factory settings.\n\nMisalignment on the WK manifests as:\n- Rapid tire wear on the inner or outer shoulder (camber out of spec)\n- Pulling to one side at highway speed (toe or caster difference side to side)\n- Vague or unsettled highway handling (caster out of spec)\n- Uneven wear across all four tires (rear toe out of spec)\n\nAny of these conditions running for tens of thousands of miles destroys expensive tires. A $100 alignment appointment prevents $600 in premature tire wear.\n\n## What to Tell the Alignment Shop\n\nBring the vehicle's lift specs and, if you installed aftermarket UCAs, the target alignment specifications from the UCA manufacturer. Generic factory specs will not be appropriate for a lifted WK.\n\nKey specifications to request:\n- **Front caster:** Increased caster (more positive) improves highway stability after a lift. [The factory specification range for caster is 3.55\u00b0 to 4.45\u00b0](https://archive.org/details/JeepGrandCherokeeWKRepairManual2005Through2010OCR7201Pages); WK lift kits and UCAs are often designed to correct caster at the target lift height.\n- **Front camber:** Should be within 0.3 degrees side to side. Negative camber (top of tire pointing inward) up to 0.5 degrees negative is acceptable.\n- **Front and rear toe:** Both should be near zero (or slightly positive for stability). Toe errors cause the fastest tire wear.\n\n## Shop Selection Matters\n\nNot all alignment shops are equipped to properly align lifted WKs. Request a shop with a Hunter 4-wheel alignment rack (with a table that reads all four corners simultaneously, not just a 2-wheel toe bar). Ask if they have experience with lifted SUVs. A 10-minute \"toe and go\" alignment for a lifted WK is not sufficient \u2014 front caster and camber require proper measurement and adjustment.\n\nAfter alignment, verify on a straight, level road that the vehicle tracks straight without pulling. If it still pulls after the alignment, return to the shop \u2014 a proper alignment corrects a pull."},{"id":"death-wobble-wk","title":"Vibration and Shimmy on the WK \u2014 It Is Not Death Wobble","vehicle":"wk","category":"steering","subcategory":"vibration","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":30,"high":350},"tools_required":["jack stands","floor jack","torque wrench","dial indicator (optional)"],"skills_required":["basic diagnosis","steering inspection"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2005-2010"],"tags":["vibration","shimmy","wheel bearing","tire balance","tie rod","IFS","steering"],"parts":[{"name":"WK front wheel bearing hub assembly (each)","vendor":"Timken or Moog","price_estimate_usd":185},{"name":"WK tie rod end (each)","vendor":"Moog ES3658 or similar","price_estimate_usd":65},{"name":"Wheel balance (per tire)","vendor":"Local tire shop","price_estimate_usd":15}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep Grand Cherokee WK repair manual 2005-2010, front suspension description and diagnosis (archive.org scan)","url":"https://archive.org/details/JeepGrandCherokeeWKRepairManual2005Through2010OCR7201Pages"}],"alternatives":["alignment-wk","lift-options-wk"],"summary":"The WK does not get death wobble. Death wobble is a specific solid-axle phenomenon caused by caster instability and worn steering components. The WK's IFS cannot produce it. What WK owners experience as \"death wobble\" is one of several separate issues, each with a distinct fix.","description":"## Death Wobble vs. IFS Shimmy\n\nTrue death wobble on a solid-axle Jeep is a violent, self-sustaining oscillation caused by the solid front axle oscillating around the caster axis. It typically occurs at a specific highway speed (often 55\u201365 mph), starts suddenly when a bump is hit, and is difficult to stop without slowing significantly. It is caused by worn or loose steering components \u2014 track bar, tie rods, ball joints, caster angle \u2014 that allow the axle to enter a resonant oscillation.\n\nThe WK has no solid front axle and cannot produce this. Each front wheel moves independently on its own control arm and strut \u2014 there is no mechanism for the oscillating feedback loop that causes death wobble.\n\nWhat WK owners experience instead falls into three distinct categories:\n\n## Category 1: Tire Balance Shimmy\n\n**Symptoms:** Vibration at a specific highway speed (commonly 65\u201375 mph), affects the steering wheel, goes away or reduces when you brake (brake torque changes the resonant frequency), and does not feel directional \u2014 the vehicle does not want to turn.\n\n**Cause:** A tire out of balance, or a wheel weight that fell off.\n\n**Fix:** Wheel balance on all four tires ($15 each). Verify with road force balancing if standard balancing doesn't resolve it. Sometimes a specific tire has a uniformity defect that cannot be corrected with weights \u2014 test by rotating the suspect tire to the rear and confirming the vibration moves.\n\n## Category 2: Wheel Bearing Wear\n\n**Symptoms:** A growling, grinding, or humming noise that changes pitch with vehicle speed and changes character when the vehicle leans into a turn (loading one side vs. the other). The steering may feel vague or loose. Wobble may be felt more continuously, not just at a specific speed.\n\n**Cause:** [The WK's front hub bearings are double-row units, sealed and lubricated for life](https://archive.org/details/JeepGrandCherokeeWKRepairManual2005Through2010OCR7201Pages), and the factory diagnosis chart lists a loose or worn wheel bearing as the first cause of front end noise. They wear with mileage. Typical service life is 80,000\u2013120,000 miles; towing, off-road use, and potholes reduce this. Grabbing the tire at 9 and 3 o'clock and feeling for play confirms a loose bearing.\n\n**Fix:** Replace the wheel bearing hub assembly on the affected side ($185 for a quality Timken or Moog unit). This is a hub removal, press-out, and install job \u2014 some shops can replace the bearing pressed into the existing hub; others replace the whole assembly. The hub assembly is the simpler approach.\n\n## Category 3: Worn Tie Rod Ends\n\n**Symptoms:** Vague or wander-y steering feel, directional instability that feels like the front end is not tracking where you point it, or a loose clunking sensation over bumps felt through the steering wheel.\n\n**Cause:** The WK's outer tie rod ends wear over time. Grab the steering linkage at the outer tie rod and test for play. Any movement that is not steering input indicates worn ends.\n\n**Fix:** Replace the tie rod ends ($65 each, Moog ES3658 or equivalent). Requires an alignment after replacement."},{"id":"lift-options-wk","title":"Lift Options and the IFS Reality \u2014 WK Grand Cherokee","vehicle":"wk","category":"suspension","subcategory":"lift","type":"reference","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":8},"cost_usd":{"low":150,"high":800},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","sockets","torque wrench","spring compressor"],"skills_required":["basic suspension","spring compressor safety"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2005-2010"],"tags":["suspension","lift","IFS","independent front suspension","spacer lift","UCA","CV angle"],"parts":[{"name":"Rough Country 2\" WK lift spacer kit","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":155},{"name":"Skyjacker 2.5\" WK lift kit (springs + struts)","vendor":"Skyjacker","price_estimate_usd":580},{"name":"Skyjacker WK upper control arms","vendor":"Skyjacker","price_estimate_usd":390},{"name":"Rubicon Express 2\" WK spacer lift","vendor":"Rubicon Express","price_estimate_usd":195}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep Grand Cherokee WK repair manual 2005-2010, Group 2 front suspension description and alignment (archive.org scan)","url":"https://archive.org/details/JeepGrandCherokeeWKRepairManual2005Through2010OCR7201Pages"}],"alternatives":["shocks-wk","uca-wk","alignment-wk"],"summary":"The WK's independent front suspension (IFS) means lift height is limited in a way that does not apply to solid-axle Jeeps. Lifting it changes the angles of the control arms and the front axle shafts, and [the factory manual sets camber and caster only at the lower control arm slots](https://archive.org/details/JeepGrandCherokeeWKRepairManual2005Through2010OCR7201Pages), so a lift without matching geometry parts leaves the alignment out of range and loads the CV joints harder. Be clear-eyed about what the WK is: a capable light trail and overland vehicle, not a rock crawler.","description":"## What IFS Means for Lift\n\nThe WK uses an independent front suspension with struts, control arms, and CV axles rather than a solid front axle. This design provides a smoother highway ride and better on-road handling than a solid-axle Jeep, but it introduces a fundamental constraint for off-road lift: the CV axle angles.\n\nWhen you lift an IFS vehicle, the control arms angle upward and the CV axles angle downward from the differential. Beyond a certain lift height, the CV joint operates at angles it was not designed for \u2014 the joint generates heat, wears its internal components rapidly, and eventually fails. On the WK, 2.5\" of lift without correcting the upper control arm geometry is the point where CV wear becomes significant.\n\nThis is not a fixable engineering problem \u2014 it is inherent to IFS. The WK will never have the articulation or the ability to run very large tires of a solid-axle Jeep, and that needs to be accepted before building the vehicle.\n\n## Lift Options\n\n**Spacer lift (1.5\u20132\"):** The simplest lift \u2014 a polyurethane or aluminum spacer sits above the strut on the front strut tower and above the coil spring perch at the rear. The Rough Country and Rubicon Express spacer kits ($150\u2013195) install in a few hours without special tools (other than a spring compressor for the front). This level of lift provides adequate clearance for 32\" tires and improved approach angle. CV angles remain within acceptable limits at 1.5\u20132\".\n\n**Strut and spring lift (2\u20132.5\"):** A quality strut and spring replacement (Skyjacker, Rancho, Fox) designed for the WK provides 2\u20132.5\" of lift with better on-road damping than spacers. The springs are engineered for the specific lift height, keeping geometry closer to spec than stacking a spacer on stock springs. This is the better choice for a WK used regularly on the highway.\n\n**Above 2.5\": UCAs required.** Going beyond 2.5\" without aftermarket upper control arms (UCAs) puts the front CV joints at angles that cause fast wear. Skyjacker makes WK-specific UCA kits for use with their 2\u20133\" lift systems. See uca-wk.\n\n## Articulation Expectations\n\nThe WK's IFS fundamentally limits articulation. A solid-axle Wrangler can articulate 20\u201325\" of wheel travel. The WK's IFS gives approximately 8\u201310\" of front wheel travel. This means on technical terrain with large rocks or deep ruts, the WK will lift a front wheel and lose traction in situations where a solid-axle Jeep keeps all four wheels on the ground. A rear locker (see rear-locker-wk) helps, but it does not solve the fundamental articulation limit.\n\nSet trail expectations accordingly. The WK is excellent on moderate trails, forest roads, and improved dirt \u2014 terrain that does not require maximum articulation. For true rock crawling with large obstacles, the WK is not the right tool."},{"id":"shocks-wk","title":"Shock Absorber Upgrade for the WK Grand Cherokee","vehicle":"wk","category":"suspension","subcategory":"shocks","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":280,"high":800},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","sockets","torque wrench","spring compressor"],"skills_required":["basic suspension","shock replacement"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2005-2010"],"tags":["shocks","struts","Bilstein","Rancho","Fox","IFS","suspension"],"parts":[{"name":"Bilstein 5100 front struts (WK 0\u20132\" lift)","vendor":"Bilstein","price_estimate_usd":185},{"name":"Rancho RS5000X front struts (WK)","vendor":"Rancho","price_estimate_usd":145},{"name":"Fox 2.0 Performance Series rear shocks (WK)","vendor":"Fox","price_estimate_usd":210},{"name":"Bilstein 5100 rear shocks (WK)","vendor":"Bilstein","price_estimate_usd":130}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep Grand Cherokee WK repair manual 2005-2010, Group 2 rear suspension: shock description and removal (archive.org scan)","url":"https://archive.org/details/JeepGrandCherokeeWKRepairManual2005Through2010OCR7201Pages"}],"alternatives":["lift-options-wk","uca-wk"],"summary":"The WK's front suspension uses struts \u2014 not traditional shocks \u2014 meaning any front damper upgrade must account for the spring. Replacing just the shock insert without addressing the spring gives a partial improvement. A full strut assembly is the correct upgrade.","description":"## Front Suspension: Struts, Not Shocks\n\nThe WK's IFS front end uses a MacPherson strut configuration: the spring and shock are combined in a single strut assembly. This means that unlike a solid-axle Jeep where you can swap shocks independently of springs, any front WK upgrade involves both the damper and the spring working together.\n\nOptions for the front:\n- **Stock-height replacement strut:** A quality strut assembly at the factory ride height \u2014 Bilstein 5100 or Rancho RS5000X \u2014 dramatically improves damping over worn OEM struts. The Bilstein 5100 is a monotube design that handles heat better than the OEM twin-tube and provides notably firmer, more controlled damping.\n- **Lift strut:** The Bilstein 5100 is also offered in WK-specific lengths for 0\u20132\" of lift. The 5100 replaces the entire strut assembly and provides the lift height built into the strut length rather than relying on a separate spacer. Clean, integrated solution.\n\nA spring compressor is required for front strut work. This is not optional \u2014 removing a strut without properly compressing the spring first can result in the spring launching violently. Use a quality spring compressor (not the small auto parts store rentals if possible) and follow the procedure carefully.\n\n## Rear Suspension: Traditional Shocks\n\nThe WK's rear suspension uses a solid rear axle (the 8.25-inch C213R or C213RE in the factory manual) with coil springs and separate shock absorbers. [The manual describes the stock rear shocks as twin-tube, low-pressure gas-charged units bolted to the body at the top and the axle brackets at the bottom](https://archive.org/details/JeepGrandCherokeeWKRepairManual2005Through2010OCR7201Pages). The rear shocks can be replaced without touching the springs, and extended-travel rear shocks are available for slightly improved wheel travel on lifted WKs.\n\n**Bilstein 5100 rear ($130 each):** A monotube replacement that improves rear damping noticeably over worn OEM shocks. Available in a lift-compatible length for WKs with 1.5\u20132\" of rear lift.\n\n**Fox 2.0 Performance Series rear ($210 each):** A step up from the Bilstein in damping quality with larger body diameter and better heat management. On a WK used for extended overland driving on rough dirt roads, the Fox 2.0 provides better ride quality under load than the Bilstein.\n\n## When to Replace\n\nWorn shocks on the WK manifest as excessive body roll on highway ramps, porpoising (repeated nose-dip-tail-dip) over speed bumps, and excessive bounce after hitting a pothole. On the trail, worn rear shocks allow the rear to hop and skip over rocks rather than maintaining contact. Most WK shocks are serviceable to 60,000\u201380,000 miles; hard-driven vehicles may need replacement sooner.\n\nReplace shocks in axle pairs \u2014 front pair together, rear pair together. Mixing old and new shocks on the same axle creates uneven damping that causes pull and erratic handling."},{"id":"uca-wk","title":"Upper Control Arms (UCA) for the Lifted WK Grand Cherokee","vehicle":"wk","category":"suspension","subcategory":"control arms","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":380,"high":650},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","sockets","torque wrench","alignment equipment"],"skills_required":["suspension geometry basics","control arm R&R"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2005-2010"],"tags":["UCA","upper control arms","IFS","geometry","CV axles","lift","alignment"],"parts":[{"name":"Skyjacker WK upper control arm kit","vendor":"Skyjacker","price_estimate_usd":390},{"name":"Rubicon Express WK UCA kit","vendor":"Rubicon Express","price_estimate_usd":420}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep Grand Cherokee WK repair manual 2005-2010, Group 2 front suspension: upper ball joint/control arm service and alignment (archive.org scan)","url":"https://archive.org/details/JeepGrandCherokeeWKRepairManual2005Through2010OCR7201Pages"}],"alternatives":["lift-options-wk","alignment-wk","shocks-wk"],"summary":"Above 2 inches of lift, the WK's factory upper control arms point the CV joint at an angle that accelerates wear. Aftermarket UCAs correct the geometry and can more than double the life of the CV axles on a lifted WK.","description":"## Why UCAs Are Necessary Above 2 Inches\n\nThe IFS upper control arm (UCA) positions the upper ball joint and, by extension, dictates the operating angle of the CV axle. At stock ride height, the geometry is designed to place the CV joint within its ideal operating angle range. Lift the vehicle 2\" or more and the strut extends, the wheel drops relative to the control arm, and the CV axle runs at a steeper angle through the inner CV joint.\n\nThe inner CV joint on the WK has a maximum designed operating angle \u2014 typically around 24\u201326 degrees before fatigue begins to accelerate significantly. A stock WK at 3\" of lift can put the inner CV joint at angles approaching or exceeding this limit, especially at full suspension droop. The result is CV joint failure well before the expected service life \u2014 sometimes within 20,000\u201330,000 miles on a lifted WK that does significant off-road travel.\n\nAftermarket UCAs address this by relocating the upper ball joint position to correct the geometry for the lifted ride height. The UCA is extended, angled, or offset to restore the CV joint angle to within the design operating range at the new lifted height.\n\n## Available Options for the WK\n\nThe WK IFS UCA aftermarket is smaller than the solid-axle Jeep market, but Skyjacker and Rubicon Express both offer WK-specific kits. These kits are designed to work with their respective lift systems \u2014 the Skyjacker UCA is engineered for use with Skyjacker's 2\u20133\" WK lift kit.\n\nThe Skyjacker kit ($390) uses heavy-wall DOM tubing and high-strength ball joints rated for the WK's front axle load. They are a bolt-in replacement for the factory UCAs using the factory mounting holes \u2014 no drilling or welding required.\n\n## Alignment After UCA Install\n\n[An alignment is mandatory after installing UCAs](https://archive.org/details/JeepGrandCherokeeWKRepairManual2005Through2010OCR7201Pages) \u2014 the factory manual ends its upper ball joint and control arm procedures with \"perform a wheel alignment,\" and it sets camber and caster at the lower control arm slots, not the upper arm, so the shop needs to know what you changed. The new control arm position changes the caster, camber, and potentially the toe \u2014 running a WK with incorrect alignment causes rapid tire wear and can create handling that feels vague or pulls at highway speed. A 4-wheel alignment on a Hunter rack (not a quick toe-only alignment) is required. Bring alignment specs \u2014 Skyjacker includes the target specifications in the UCA kit for the lifted configuration.\n\nDo not drive the vehicle more than necessary between UCA install and alignment. Schedule the alignment the same day if possible."},{"id":"tire-sizing-wk","title":"Tire Fitment Guide for the IFS WK Grand Cherokee","vehicle":"wk","category":"wheels_tires","subcategory":"tire sizing","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":700,"high":1600},"tools_required":["torque wrench","floor jack","tape measure"],"skills_required":["basic wheel knowledge"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2005-2010"],"tags":["tires","tire sizing","IFS","fitment","33s","fender trimming","CV angles"],"parts":[{"name":"BFGoodrich KO2 285/70R17 (set of 4)","vendor":"BFGoodrich","price_estimate_usd":265},{"name":"Falken Wildpeak AT3W 265/70R17 (set of 4)","vendor":"Falken","price_estimate_usd":210},{"name":"Nitto Ridge Grappler 285/70R17 (set of 4)","vendor":"Nitto","price_estimate_usd":280}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep Grand Cherokee WK repair manual 2005-2010, Group 22 tire sizes (archive.org scan)","url":"https://archive.org/details/JeepGrandCherokeeWKRepairManual2005Through2010OCR7201Pages"}],"alternatives":["wheels-wk","lift-options-wk"],"summary":"[The factory manual lists P235/65R17, P245/65R17 and 245/65R17 tires for the WK](https://archive.org/details/JeepGrandCherokeeWKRepairManual2005Through2010OCR7201Pages), and anything bigger is up to you and your lift. This is where being honest about the WK's IFS matters most: the independent front suspension limits tire size in ways a solid-axle Jeep does not have. A 33x12.5 with a proper 2.5\\\" lift is achievable and practical. Going to 35s on a street-driven WK introduces problems that outweigh the benefit.","description":"## Stock Tire Size\n\nThe WK left the factory with 265/65R17 (approximately 30.6\" diameter) on most trims, and 265/70R17 (approximately 31.6\") on some Overland and Limited versions. These sizes represent the largest that fit without any modification.\n\n## With 1.5\\\" Lift: 265/70R17 or 275/65R18\n\nA minimal spacer lift (1.5\") clears 265/70R17 on factory-width wheels. If you move to aftermarket 17x9\" wheels, 275/65R18 (approximately 32\") may fit with careful monitoring of inner fender clearance. The front inner fender plastic will likely require minor trimming at the front edge where the tire turns to full lock.\n\n## With 2.5\\\" Lift: 285/70R17 Is the Target\n\nA proper 2.5\" lift kit (Skyjacker or Rough Country with UCAs) combined with 17x9\" wheels at 4.5\" backspacing is the combination owners commonly run for 285/70R17 (approximately 32.7\") \u2014 confirm clearance with your lift maker before buying. This is the sweet spot for the WK: a real-world improvement in ground clearance and traction footprint without requiring aggressive fender modification or creating on-road handling problems.\n\nAt this size, check inner fender clearance at full suspension droop with the steering at full lock \u2014 have a second person turn the wheel while you watch the tire path. Trim any plastic that contacts. No metal cutting should be required.\n\n**Note on CV angles:** At 2.5\" lift without UCAs, the CV angles are marginally acceptable but at the edge of the design range. Install UCAs (see uca-wk) for any lift at or above 2.5\" \u2014 this protects the CV axles and extends their service life significantly.\n\n## 33x12.5 on 2.5\\\" Lift with Fender Trimming\n\nA 33x12.5 tire on a 17x9\" wheel with 4.5\" backspacing fits on a 2.5\" lift WK with inner fender trimming \u2014 typically 1\u20132\" of inner plastic at the rear of the front wheel well. This is achievable without cutting sheetmetal, and the result is a WK that runs a true 33\" tire suitable for moderate trail use.\n\nThis is the practical maximum for a WK that is also daily driven. At 33\", the CV angles are stressed but manageable with UCAs installed. Tread wear on the highway is normal. Regearing is not required but is recommended \u2014 the WK's factory 3.55 or 3.73 gears with 33s cause the engine to feel slightly breathless at highway speed.\n\n## 35\\\" Tires: Why It Is Not Recommended\n\nA 35\" tire on the WK requires more aggressive inner fender cutting (into the sheetmetal), a lift beyond 3\" (which severely stresses CV angles even with UCAs), and significantly different backspacing that pushes the tire outside the fender line. The result is a vehicle that looks trail-tough but has CV axles under constant fatigue stress, alignment that is difficult to keep correct, and fenders that collect trail debris. For a daily driver, 35s on a WK create maintenance and tire wear problems that a 35-tire Wrangler or solid-axle truck would not have.\n\nIf 35s are the goal, the correct platform is a JK or JL Wrangler, not the WK."},{"id":"wheels-wk","title":"Wheel Selection for the WK Grand Cherokee","vehicle":"wk","category":"wheels_tires","subcategory":"wheels","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":280,"high":1000},"tools_required":["torque wrench","lug wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wheel knowledge"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2005-2010"],"tags":["wheels","bolt pattern","5x5","5x127","backspacing","Method","Black Rhino","IFS"],"parts":[{"name":"Method 305 NV 17x9 (5x5\", 4.5\" BS)","vendor":"Method Race Wheels","price_estimate_usd":225},{"name":"Black Rhino Armory 17x9 (5x5\")","vendor":"Black Rhino","price_estimate_usd":195},{"name":"Fuel Anza D557 17x9 (5x5\")","vendor":"Fuel Off-Road","price_estimate_usd":185}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep Grand Cherokee wheel bolt patterns by generation (WK 2005-2010: 5x127, 1/2-20 lug nuts) \u2014 Cararac","url":"https://cararac.com/bolts_sizes/jeep/grand-cherokee.html"}],"alternatives":["tire-sizing-wk"],"summary":"[The WK uses 5x5\" (5x127mm)](https://cararac.com/bolts_sizes/jeep/grand-cherokee.html) \u2014 NOT the 5x4.5\" (5x114.3mm) used on the TJ Wrangler and the ZJ. This is the most common wheel mistake made by WK buyers. Confirm the bolt pattern before ordering.","description":"## Bolt Pattern: 5x5\" (5x127mm)\n\nThe WK Grand Cherokee (2005\u20132010) uses a **5x5\" (5x127mm)** bolt pattern. This is shared with some other Jeep vehicles (including the WK2 Grand Cherokee) but is NOT the same as the 5x4.5\" used on:\n- Jeep Wrangler TJ (1997\u20132006)\n- ZJ Grand Cherokee (1993\u20131998)\n- XJ Cherokee\n\nThe WJ Grand Cherokee and the JK and JL Wranglers do use 5x5\", though center bore and backspacing differ, so check both before borrowing a wheel.\n\nThe difference between 5x4.5\" and 5x5\" is small enough that a wheel from the wrong bolt pattern might appear to fit when set on the hub, but it will not center correctly and the lug pattern is misaligned. Torquing lug nuts on a wrong-pattern wheel deforms the lugs and can result in the wheel coming off.\n\n**Always verify bolt pattern before purchasing.** Use a bolt pattern gauge or measure center-to-center across opposite studs: 4.5\" across opposite studs = 5x4.5\". 5\" across opposite studs = 5x5\".\n\nThe WK hub bore is 71.5mm \u2014 the same as the ZJ and WJ, and compatible with most WK-specific aftermarket wheels.\n\n## Width and Backspacing\n\nFor 285/70R17 (approximately 32.7\") tires on a 2.5\" lifted WK:\n- **Width:** 17x9\" is the standard choice. The extra width improves tire profile on a 285mm tire.\n- **Backspacing:** 4.5\" on a 17x9\" positions the tire correctly within the wheel well on a properly lifted WK. This keeps the tire inside the fender line while clearing the inner fender at full droop and full steering lock.\n\nGoing to 3.75\" backspacing pushes the tire outboard and can create clearance issues at the front bumper corner area on the WK's body-colored plastic. Going to 5\" backspacing tucks the tire too far inboard and risks contact with the front spring/strut at full droop.\n\n## Weight and IFS Handling\n\nWheel weight matters more on an IFS vehicle than on a solid-axle Jeep. Unsprung mass (wheel, tire, brake rotor) affects ride quality and handling more significantly when each front wheel moves independently. A heavy alloy wheel \u2014 30+ lbs \u2014 on the WK's IFS front end degrades ride quality noticeably compared to a lighter wheel.\n\nThe Method 305 NV, despite being a robust trail wheel, runs about 24\u201326 lbs in 17x9\" \u2014 reasonable for the WK. Avoid heavy decorative alloys designed for street use on lifted trucks; they look the part but make the WK's IFS feel heavy and sluggish.\n\n## Recommended Wheels\n\n**Method 305 NV 17x9\" in 5x5\" ($225):** The correct 5x5\" bolt pattern, a purpose-built trail wheel with reinforced spoke base, and a manageable weight. Available with black or machined finish.\n\n**Black Rhino Armory 17x9\" in 5x5\" ($195):** An aggressive-looking trail wheel at a lower price point. Slightly heavier than the Method.\n\n**Fuel Anza D557 17x9\" in 5x5\" ($185):** A mid-tier option with a clean street-to-trail aesthetic. Fuel Off-Road has a wide selection in the 5x5\" bolt pattern specifically."}];